---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
---

<Base title="Privacy Policy - Pikos" description="Pikos privacy policy. Your data stays on your device. Plain language, no legalese.">

  <main class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-8 py-20">

    <!-- Back to home -->
    <a href="/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-neutral-400 transition-colors hover:text-primary-500 dark:text-neutral-500 dark:hover:text-primary-400">
      <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m15 18-6-6 6-6"/></svg>
      Home
    </a>

    <h1 class="mt-8 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-neutral-400 dark:text-neutral-500">Effective: August 5, 2026</p>

    <div class="mt-10 space-y-6 text-base leading-relaxed text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-300">

      <p>
        This policy is short because there isn't much to say.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Your data stays on your device</h2>

      <p>
        Everything you create in Pikos (notes, tasks, calendar events, folders, and settings) is stored in a standard, open database format on your machine. There is no sign-up and no way for us to see what you write. You can copy, back up, or move your data at any time; there is no lock-in.
      </p>

      <p>
        We cannot access, read, or recover your data. If you delete the app, the data goes with it unless you've backed it up yourself.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Network connections</h2>

      <p>
        The app's only built-in network request is an update check when you launch it. Nothing about you or your data is sent, and you can turn it off in Settings → General.
      </p>

      <p>
        Any feature that needs the network is opt-in, a toggle in Settings. Calendar sync, below, is the first one. If you turn a feature on, the relevant third-party provider's privacy policy governs whatever data flows through them.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">External calendar sync</h2>

      <p>
        Pikos can show events from an outside calendar: iCloud, Google Calendar, or any CalDAV server. This is opt-in and off by default. Nothing connects until you add an account in Settings and turn a calendar on.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sync is read-only and one-way. Pikos downloads your events into the same local database as everything else, so they work offline. Nothing you write in Pikos is sent to your calendar provider, and nothing is sent to us. The only network traffic is between your device and the provider you connected.
      </p>

      <p>
        Pikos's use of information received from Google APIs adheres to the <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy" class="text-primary-500 hover:underline">Google API Services User Data Policy</a>, including the Limited Use requirements. In practice: your Google Calendar data shows up in the app, stays on your device, and goes nowhere else. Not to us, not to advertisers.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">How your data is protected</h2>

      <p>
        Your calendar data, Google's included, travels straight from your device to the provider over an encrypted connection (HTTPS). It isn't routed through us, and there's no Pikos server that could hold a copy of it.
      </p>

      <p>
        On your machine it's stored in the app's database, inside your own user account and protected by the same permissions that protect the rest of your files. If you have full-disk encryption turned on, it's encrypted on disk too. Sign-in tokens and app passwords are kept apart from all of that, in your operating system's keychain, which encrypts them. They're never stored in the database and never written to logs.
      </p>

      <p>
        Pikos only ever reads. It asks Google for read-only access to your calendar list and your events, and nothing else: it can't create, change, or delete anything in your Google Calendar.
      </p>

      <p>
        Your Google data stays only as long as you keep the account connected. Disconnect it and Pikos deletes the stored tokens, revokes its access with Google, and removes the events that came from that account. You can revoke access yourself at any time from your <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions" class="text-primary-500 hover:underline">Google account permissions page</a>. Delete the app and everything goes with it. Pages you've written on stay, because they're yours.
      </p>

      <p>
        We don't sell your data, share it with third parties, or use it for advertising. Google user data is never used to develop, improve, or train AI or machine-learning models of any kind.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Website analytics</h2>

      <p>
        This website uses privacy-focused analytics to count page views and downloads. No cookies, no fingerprinting, no cross-site tracking. We see aggregate numbers: how many people visit each page and download the app. We cannot identify individual visitors.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Changes to this policy</h2>

      <p>
        If this policy changes, we'll update the date at the top. For material changes, like collecting new data, we'll note it clearly here and in the app.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Contact</h2>

      <p>
        Questions about privacy? Email <a href="mailto:hello@pikos.app" class="text-primary-500 hover:underline">hello@pikos.app</a>.
      </p>

    </div>

  </main>

  <Footer current="/privacy" />
</Base>

<style>

  </style>
